1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,700
A puddle disappears
after a sunny afternoon.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:07,300
Later, rain fills the same hollow again.

3
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:10,400
Water has moved, but much
of its journey

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,200
happened where we could not see it.

5
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,800
To understand where rain comes from,
we need to follow water

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,000
through the air, inside a cloud,
and back onto the ground.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,700
Start with water at the surface.

8
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:31,000
Energy from the sun helps liquid water
change into a gas called water vapor.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,600
This is evaporation.

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:37,200
It happens at the surface
of oceans, lakes, and rivers,

11
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,000
and it can happen in that puddle too.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The water leaves the surface
and enters the surrounding air.

13
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,400
The puddle does not need to boil.

14
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:54,400
Water can evaporate at temperatures
well below its boiling point.

15
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,400
So the absence of bubbles
does not mean the water is staying put.

16
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:03,300
A surface can gradually lose water
while the change

17
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,000
remains invisible to someone
standing beside it.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Plants provide another
route into the air.

19
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,300
Their roots take up liquid water
from the soil.

20
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:19,500
Water moves through the plant, and some
is released from the leaves as vapor.

21
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:22,100
This process is called transpiration.

22
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:24,100
A patch of growing vegetation

23
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:27,000
is therefore part of the movement
of water,

24
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
not just a background to it.

25
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:33,800
We can make that normally
hidden movement easier to notice.

26
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,900
Place a clear plastic bag
around some leaves,

27
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,550
and moisture can collect
on its inside.

28
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:43,950
The water released by the leaves
has changed back into droplets.

29
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:49,000
What was difficult to see in
the open air becomes visible on the bag.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Once water is in the atmosphere,
moving air can carry it to other places.

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,400
The water that eventually falls
over land does not

32
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,900
have to come from the ground
immediately beneath it.

33
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,200
Winds transport moisture,

34
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:09,100
linking the surface where
water entered the

35
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:12,000
air with places where it
may later return.

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
To form a cloud,
moist air often rises and cools.

37
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Higher in the atmosphere,
the surrounding pressure is lower.

38
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,500
A rising portion of air expands,
and that expansion cools it.

39
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:29,600
This is an important step:
a change in the air's temperature

40
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:33,500
can change what happens
to the water vapor within it.

41
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,500
As the air cools enough,
water vapor can become liquid droplets.

42
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,000
The change from gas to liquid
is condensation.

43
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,100
You can see another example
on the outside of a cold glass.

44
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:50,300
Water from the surrounding air
collects on the cool surface,

45
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,300
forming small drops that may join
and run down its sides.

46
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:57,300
In the atmosphere,
tiny particles of dust,

47
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:00,250
salt, or other material
help droplets form.

48
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:02,050
Water gathers around them.

49
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:05,800
A cloud contains these small droplets,
or ice crystals,

50
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:09,650
rather than simply being a patch
of visible water vapor.

51
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:15,000
The cloud we can see is evidence that
water has changed its physical form.

52
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,300
Cloud formation
is not a permanent finish line.

53
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:22,300
Droplets can also evaporate again
when conditions change.

54
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:25,600
A cloud's outline can grow,
shrink, or disappear

55
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:28,700
as water changes between
vapor and droplets.

56
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:32,800
The visible shape tells us something
about conditions in the

57
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,800
air, rather than marking
a fixed container full of water.

58
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,500
Having a cloud does not automatically
give us rain on the ground.

59
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,600
Its tiny droplets or ice crystals
must grow

60
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:47,400
large enough to fall
as precipitation.

61
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,150
That word includes rain, snow,
and other

62
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:52,500
forms of water falling from clouds.

63
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:57,300
The next part of the story is
therefore about growth inside the cloud.

64
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,400
One route begins when droplets
of different sizes collide.

65
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,700
If they join, a larger drop forms.

66
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:06,500
More collisions
can make it larger still.

67
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,350
This process is called coalescence,
which simply means joining together.

68
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:15,300
A raindrop develops through changes
inside the cloud; it is

69
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:18,250
not a tiny cloud falling
out of the sky.

70
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:21,750
There is another route
in colder clouds that

71
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:25,000
contain both liquid droplets
and ice crystals.

72
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,200
Water vapor can add directly to the ice,
allowing the crystals to grow.

73
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:34,700
Rain formation is therefore
not limited to liquid droplets joining.

74
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:40,000
The conditions within a cloud determine
which growth processes can take place.

75
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,480
The movement of air matters too.

76
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:46,380
Mountains can force air upward
as it meets rising terrain.

77
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:50,230
A weather front can also lift
warmer air over colder air.

78
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:55,280
These provide ways to begin the upward
movement that helps cloud formation.

79
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:58,220
We need to consider what the air
is doing,

80
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:01,000
as well as how much
moisture it contains.

81
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,950
Thunderstorms need a particular
combination:

82
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:07,370
moisture, unstable air,
and a way to lift that air.

83
00:05:07,370 --> 00:05:11,320
A supply of water vapor alone
is not the whole explanation.

84
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:16,620
This is why understanding a storm means
looking at several conditions together,

85
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:19,540
rather than assuming that one
warm or humid

86
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:22,000
afternoon guarantees a thunderstorm.

87
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:26,700
Now follow the rain
after it reaches the ground.

88
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:31,300
Some water moves across the surface,
flowing downhill under gravity.

89
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:34,800
Small flows can join channels,
streams, and rivers.

90
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:36,400
This is surface runoff.

91
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:40,100
The rain we noticed at one spot
may become moving water

92
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:44,000
farther away, connecting
different parts of the landscape.

93
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:47,000
Some water takes a less visible route.

94
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,750
It enters openings in the soil and rock,
a process called infiltration.

95
00:05:51,750 --> 00:05:54,650
Part can remain in shallow soil,
while part

96
00:05:54,650 --> 00:05:57,600
travels deeper
and replenishes groundwater.

97
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:01,100
Rain reaching a field does not
all follow one path:

98
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:04,700
some stays near the surface,
and some moves below it.

99
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:07,000
Underground water
usually does not

100
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:08,700
occupy a vast open river

101
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:09,900
beneath our feet.

102
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:14,000
Much of it fills spaces between
particles and cracks in rock.

103
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,820
Gravity and pressure help move it
through those openings.

104
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:22,000
The speed and direction depend
on the material, so its journey

105
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,100
can differ greatly from water
flowing along a visible stream.

106
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:30,900
Groundwater can eventually emerge
into rivers, springs, or the ocean.

107
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:35,900
Some travels relatively quickly; some
remains underground for much longer.

108
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:39,100
A river's water can therefore
include water that

109
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,000
took an underground route
before arriving.

110
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,700
What we see flowing at the surface
may have a history

111
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:48,700
that extends well beyond
the latest rainfall.

112
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,950
The condition of the ground
affects how these paths divide.

113
00:06:52,950 --> 00:06:56,830
Soil that is already wet
may accept less additional water.

114
00:06:56,830 --> 00:07:00,580
Different soils also let water
enter at different rates.

115
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:03,790
The amount and intensity of rain
matter as well.

116
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:09,000
Counting the rain alone does not tell
us how much water will enter the ground.

117
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,700
Roads, roofs,
and other surfaces

118
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:14,200
that water cannot easily pass through

119
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:15,800
change the route again.

120
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,150
More rain can move quickly into
drains and streams

121
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:21,700
instead of soaking into the ground.

122
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:25,000
Building across a landscape
can therefore change

123
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,200
how water moves after a storm,

124
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,600
even though the process began

125
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:32,000
with rain falling from clouds.

126
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,300
These different paths
belong to the water cycle.

127
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:40,200
Water is stored in the atmosphere,
at the surface, and below ground.

128
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:45,000
It moves between those places and
changes between liquid, gas, and ice.

129
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,650
The cycle is not a rule that
every drop must visit

130
00:07:48,650 --> 00:07:52,000
every stage in exactly
the same order.

131
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:54,800
It also explains why
water availability

132
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:58,000
depends on more than whether
it has rained.

133
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:03,000
Where water is stored, when it moves,
and how clean it is all matter.

134
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,750
Water arriving at the surface
still needs

135
00:08:05,750 --> 00:08:08,700
to reach a useful place
at a useful time.

136
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:10,880
Understanding its route helps us

137
00:08:10,880 --> 00:08:13,900
understand the supply
we actually depend on.

138
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:15,650
Return to the puddle.

139
00:08:15,650 --> 00:08:19,950
Its disappearance was one visible part
of a much larger process.

140
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:22,750
Rain brought water back
to the surface

141
00:08:22,750 --> 00:08:26,250
after movement and changes
in the atmosphere.

142
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:30,000
Some of that water will flow away,

143
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:34,250
some will enter the soil,
and some will return to the air.

144
00:08:34,250 --> 00:08:39,000
An ordinary rainy day lets us watch
part of that continuing movement.
